**Note:** Docker Compose v2 was successfully installed, but the Docker daemon returned a permission error. User permissions needed to be configured to allow Docker commands without `sudo`.
    -->sudo systemctl status docker
    -->sudo systemctl start docker
    -->sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
    -->newgrp docker
    -->docker info
    -->**Expected Return:** Docker Client and Server information without permission errors.

**Note:** Docker permissions were successfully configured. The VM was initially allocated insufficient resources for Apache Superset. Memory allocation was increased through Oracle VM VirtualBox under Settings → System → Motherboard → Base Memory (8192 MB).

    -->free -h
    -->**Expected Return:** Approximately 7.8 GiB total memory.
    -->nproc
    -->**Expected Return:** Number of allocated virtual processors.
    -->df -h

**Note:** With Docker Compose v2 operational and VM memory increased, Apache Superset installation can resume from the existing repository directory.

    -->cd ~/superset
    -->ls docker-compose*.yml
    -->docker compose -f docker-compose-image-tag.yml up
    -->**Expected Return:** Docker begins pulling container images, creating containers, and initializing Superset services.

    **Note:** Once the containers have initialized, verify that Apache Superset is operational. Run the following commands in a separate terminal while the Compose process remains active.

     -->cd ~/superset
     -->docker ps
     -->docker compose -f docker-compose-image-tag.yml ps
     -->**Expected Return:** Superset and its required supporting containers are running.

    **Note:** After successful container initialization, access the Apache Superset web interface to validate deployment.

     -->http://localhost:8088
     -->**Expected Return:** Apache Superset login interface.
 


